    "summary": "Cloth (Yoruba aso oke), a single strip hand-woven from imported industrial cotton. The warp is formed of narrow stripes of brown and white cotton of equal width arranged alternately. The ground weft is of white cotton, and a pattern is formed by running discontinuous supplementary threads of green rayon across the surface of the cloth. These each have triangles flanked by lines on all four sides, and are woven in six identical blocks that run along the strip at regular intervals. The warp ends have been twisted to form tassels.",
